# Shell scripts must stay LF — CRLF breaks bash (the Stop hook runs via bash)
*.sh text eol=lf

# The macOS launcher is a shell script, and core.autocrlf=true on the machine
# that wrote it: without this rule a fresh checkout hands macOS a CRLF
# .command, and bash dies on the first line with "$'\r': command not found".
# The shipped zip is built from the worktree, so this is what keeps a clone -
# or a macOS CI runner - from producing a launcher nobody can run.
*.command text eol=lf

# Batch files must be CRLF — cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only .bat/.cmd (labels and
# goto in particular), and the launcher ships to users inside a zip.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary asset: never text-convert an icon.
*.ico binary
